      "card_ids": ["C06"],
      "claim": "In a rabbit S. pneumoniae meningitis model, oritavancin CSF penetration was only 5% of plasma levels, demonstrating that even with meningitis-induced BBB disruption, this glycopeptide class has poor CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "Oritavancin showed limited 1–5% CSF penetration in a rabbit meningitis model, directly relevant to the query's meningitis condition.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a large, highly polar glycopeptide; direct evidence from closely related lipoglycopeptides (dalbavancin, oritavancin) shows negligible-to-low CSF penetration even in meningitis, so meaningful CNS access is unlikely.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct CSF or brain exposure data for the exact query molecule.",
    "Rabbit meningitis models may not fully reflect human BBB disruption or transport.",
    "Unbound brain concentrations and contributions of active uptake/efflux were not measured."
  ],
